@*
    The whole instruction folder read twice — once as Claude Code would read it,
    once as GitHub Copilot would — rather than one file read closely. That is the
    question this view answers and the file list next to it cannot: a file says
    what it governs, and says nothing at all about whether the assistant you are
    talking to will ever see it.

    A table rather than a chart. The reading is ordinal and two columns wide, so
    a shade scale would spend a legend on four values a word says outright, and
    the words are what a reader takes away — "not read" is the finding, not a
    pale cell.

    Rows are discovery's order, which is the file list's order, so the two
    surfaces can be read against each other.
*@

<div class="instruction-reach" data-testid="@TestId">
    <p class="instruction-reach__intro">
        <strong>Always</strong> is every session. <strong>On match</strong> is only when a path in the
        stated scope is touched. <strong>Linked</strong> means a file the host does load names this one
        in prose, so it can be opened but is not loaded. <strong>Not read</strong> means the host has no
        path to it.
    </p>

    @if (OneSided.Count > 0)
    {
        <p class="instruction-reach__intro">
            <strong>@OneSided.Count of @Comparison.Rows.Count</strong>
            @(OneSided.Count == 1 ? "file reaches" : "files reach") one assistant and effectively not the
            other. A rule that governs the repository for one and does not exist for the other drifts
            without anybody noticing, because each host is behaving correctly on its own side of it.
        </p>
    }

    @* What each host is carrying, which is the question the sizes are here to
       answer. Two readings: with nothing picked it is the resting cost of opening
       a session, and with paths picked it is what working on those files comes
       to. *@
    <div class="instruction-reach__totals">
        <p class="instruction-reach__totals-title">
            @(Comparison.IsScoped
                ? $"Loaded when working on {Comparison.Paths.Count} selected {(Comparison.Paths.Count == 1 ? "path" : "paths")}"
                : "Loaded in every session, before any file is opened")
        </p>

        <MetricGrid MinColumnWidth="13rem" Label="Context each assistant carries" TestId="instructions-reach-totals">
            <MetricTile Label="Claude Code"
                        Value="@Kilobytes(Comparison.Claude.Bytes)"
                        Unit="KB"
                        Footnote="@Breakdown(Comparison.Claude)"
                        TestId="instructions-reach-total-claude" />
            <MetricTile Label="GitHub Copilot"
                        Value="@Kilobytes(Comparison.Copilot.Bytes)"
                        Unit="KB"
                        Footnote="@Breakdown(Comparison.Copilot)"
                        TestId="instructions-reach-total-copilot" />
        </MetricGrid>

        @* The token figure is an estimate and says so wherever it appears; the
           line figure is exact and still the weakest of the three, so the one
           thing a reader must not do with it is rank files. Both cautions belong
           next to the numbers rather than in a document nobody opens. *@
        <p class="instruction-reach__caveat">
            Tokens are estimated at four bytes each, not counted — no tokenizer ships with this app.
            Lines are exact but measure formatting rather than content: in this repository
            <code>copilot-instructions.md</code> has fewer lines than
            <code>storybook.instructions.md</code> and more bytes, so lines rank the two backwards.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* The same figures as a share of the window they are spent out of, which is
       the question the totals above only half answer: 25 KB means nothing until
       you know what it is 25 KB of.

       The fourth part is the one to be careful with. Claude Code's own context
       view calls its remainder "free space"; this one cannot, because messages,
       tool definitions, skills and the system prompt all live in there and this
       app can see none of them. Naming it for what it is keeps the bar from
       claiming the window is emptier than it is — on the one surface built to be
       trusted about exactly that. *@
    <div class="instruction-reach__window">
        <div class="instruction-reach__window-head">
            <p class="instruction-reach__totals-title">Against the context window</p>

            <SelectField CurrentValue="@_window.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                         Options="@WindowOptions"
                         Label="Context window"
                         TestId="instructions-reach-window"
                         OnChanged="SelectWindow" />
        </div>

        @* Named, because the legend is the content and the label above it is a
           bare paragraph the list has no link to. Read by list, every row would
           otherwise arrive without saying which assistant it belonged to. *@
        <MetricBreakdownBar Parts="@WindowParts(row => row.Claude, "Claude Code")"
                            Label="Claude Code"
                            FormatValue="@FormatTokens"
                            TestId="instructions-reach-window-claude"
                            role="group"
                            aria-label="Claude Code context window" />

        <MetricBreakdownBar Parts="@WindowParts(row => row.Copilot, "GitHub Copilot")"
                            Label="GitHub Copilot"
                            FormatValue="@FormatTokens"
                            TestId="instructions-reach-window-copilot"
                            role="group"
                            aria-label="GitHub Copilot context window" />

        @* Said out loud, because a reader who picks a path and sees one of two
           bars move will read the still one as broken. It is not: a host with no
           path-scoped rules has nothing for a path to switch on. *@
        @if (Comparison.IsScoped && HasNoPathScopedRules(row => row.Claude))
        {
            <p class="instruction-reach__caveat" data-testid="instructions-reach-window-note">
                Claude Code's bar does not move when you pick a path: nothing it reads in this
                repository is scoped by one, so there is nothing for a path to switch on.
            </p>
        }

        <p class="instruction-reach__caveat">
            Every figure here is the same four-bytes-per-token estimate as above.
            <strong>The last band is not free space.</strong> It holds the conversation, the tool
            definitions, the skills and the system prompt — none of which this app can see, so what
            is left of the window is smaller than that band suggests.
        </p>
    </div>

    @* What the repository cannot tell you. Everything above is checked in and the
       same for everybody; this is read off the machine, so it differs per
       developer and is loaded all the same. Kept as its own block with its own
       subtotal rather than folded into the figures above, because those are a
       fact about the repository and these are a fact about one laptop. *@
    @if (UserContext.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="instruction-reach__machine" data-testid="instructions-reach-machine">
            <p class="instruction-reach__totals-title">
                Loaded from this machine, outside the repository
            </p>

            <DataTable TItem="UserContextSource"
                       Columns="@MachineColumns"
                       Items="@UserContext"
                       RowKey="@MachineKey"
                       AriaLabel="Context each assistant loads from this machine"
                       EmptyMessage="No user-level locations were checked."
                       RowNoun="location"
                       TestId="instructions-reach-machine-table">
                <Row Context="source">
                    <th scope="row" class="instruction-reach__file">
                        @source.Label
                        <span class="instruction-reach__scope">@source.Path</span>
                    </th>
                    <td>@source.Host</td>
                    <td class="instruction-reach__size" data-testid="instructions-reach-machine-state">
                        @if (source.State == UserContextState.Present)
                        {
                            @($"{source.Files} {(source.Files == 1 ? "file" : "files")}")<br />
                            @Kilobytes(source.Bytes)

                            <text> KB</text>

                            <br />
                            <span class="instruction-reach__estimate">~@source.EstimatedTokens tokens est.</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <Badge Kind="reach" Slug="not-read" Title="@MachineExplain(source)">@MachineLabel(source)</Badge>
                        }
                    </td>
                </Row>
            </DataTable>

            <p class="instruction-reach__caveat">
                Read from this machine's home directory, so it is not in the repository and the next
                developer's figures will differ. Only paths and sizes are read here — never the contents.
                Project memory counts its index alone: the notes beside it are recalled when something
                makes them relevant, not carried into every session.
                <strong>With it, Claude Code carries @Kilobytes(Comparison.Claude.Bytes + MachineTotal("Claude Code").Bytes) KB
                and GitHub Copilot @Kilobytes(Comparison.Copilot.Bytes + MachineTotal("GitHub Copilot").Bytes) KB.</strong>
            </p>
        </div>
    }

    @* The picker. Folded away by default: it is a question a reader asks
       sometimes, and the table under it is what they came for.

       A ToggleButton and not a `details` element. The library owns the pressed
       control the rest of this panel already uses, and a disclosure widget the
       screen drew itself would be the second implementation of a thing the
       library has — the same rule that put the shared file view here. *@
    <div class="instruction-reach__picker">
        <ToggleButton BaseClass="instruction-reach__picker-toggle"
                      PressedCssClass="instruction-reach__picker-toggle--open"
                      Pressed="@_picking"
                      TestId="instructions-reach-picker-toggle"
                      PressedChanged="value => _picking = value">
            Pick paths to see what a change would load
        </ToggleButton>

        @if (Comparison.Paths.Count > 0)
        {
            <ul class="instruction-reach__picked" aria-label="Selected paths">
                @foreach (var path in Comparison.Paths)
                {
                    var picked = path;

                    <li>
                        @* Named for what the button does, not for what it holds.
                           Without this its accessible name is the path and a
                           cross, which says nothing about pressing it. *@
                        <Badge Kind="glob"
                               Title="@($"Stop asking about {picked}")"
                               TestId="instructions-reach-picked"
                               aria-label="@($"Remove {picked} from the selection")"
                               OnClick="() => OnPathForgotten.InvokeAsync(picked)">@picked ✕</Badge>
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>

            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                       TestId="instructions-reach-clear"
                       OnClick="() => OnSelectionCleared.InvokeAsync()">Clear selection</AppButton>
        }

        @if (_picking)
        {
            @* The default class prefix, which is the knowledge menu's. This is a
               knowledge menu — the same rows, in the same pane, doing the same
               job — so a second prefix would be a second copy of that styling to
               keep in step for no difference a reader could see. *@
            <TreeView Nodes="@Tree"
                      ShowHeading="false"
                      ShowOpenButton="false"
                      NavClass="instruction-reach__tree"
                      NavAriaLabel="Repository paths"
                      TreeAriaLabel="Repository paths"
                      EmptyMessage="No local clone to pick paths from."
                      IsNodeSelected="@IsPathPicked"
                      IsNodeExpanded="@IsFolderOpen"
                      NodeClass="@PickedClass"
                      OnNodeSelected="@OnPathToggled" />
        }
    </div>

    <DataTable TItem="InstructionLoadingRow"
               Columns="@Columns"
               Items="@Comparison.Rows"
               RowKey="@RowKey"
               RowCssClass="@RowCssClass"
               AriaLabel="How each assistant loads this repository's instruction files"
               EmptyMessage="No instruction files were discovered."
               RowNoun="file"
               TestId="instructions-reach">
        <Row Context="row">
            @* The row header, not another cell. This is the name every badge in
               the row is a statement about, so a reader arriving at one of them
               out of context has to be told which file it belongs to. *@
            <th scope="row" class="instruction-reach__file">@FilePath(row)</th>
            <td class="instruction-reach__size" data-testid="instructions-reach-size">
                @row.Size.Lines lines<br />
                @Kilobytes(row.Size.Bytes) KB<br />
                <span class="instruction-reach__estimate">~@row.Size.EstimatedTokens tokens est.</span>
            </td>
            <td data-testid="instructions-reach-claude">
                <Badge Kind="reach" Slug="@Slug(row.Claude)" Title="@Explain(row.Claude)">@Label(row.Claude)</Badge>
                @if (row.Claude.Detail is { Length: > 0 } claudeDetail)
                {
                    <span class="instruction-reach__scope">@claudeDetail</span>
                }
            </td>
            <td data-testid="instructions-reach-copilot">
                <Badge Kind="reach" Slug="@Slug(row.Copilot)" Title="@Explain(row.Copilot)">@Label(row.Copilot)</Badge>
                @if (row.Copilot.Detail is { Length: > 0 } copilotDetail)
                {
                    <span class="instruction-reach__scope">@copilotDetail</span>
                }
            </td>
        </Row>
    </DataTable>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DataTableColumn> Columns =
    [
        new("File"),
        new("Size"),
        new("Claude Code"),
        new("GitHub Copilot")
    ];

    /// <summary>Every discovered file with both verdicts and both totals.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public InstructionLoadingComparison Comparison { get; set; } = InstructionLoadingComparison.Empty;

    /// <summary>The repository to pick paths out of.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<TreeNode> Tree { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The user-level locations each host reads from this machine.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<UserContextSource> UserContext { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter]
    public Func<TreeNode, bool> IsPathPicked { get; set; } = static _ => false;

    [Parameter]
    public Func<TreeNode, bool> IsFolderOpen { get; set; } = static _ => false;

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TreeNode> OnPathToggled { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnPathForgotten { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnSelectionCleared { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the picker is open. The picked paths live on the panel,
    /// because they change what it computes; whether the tree is on screen is
    /// this view's own business and nothing above it needs to know.</summary>
    private bool _picking;

    /// <summary>The window every share below is a share of. Claude Code's default
    /// today, and switchable because the reader's own is the only one that makes
    /// the percentages mean anything.</summary>
    private long _window = 200_000;

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> WindowOptions =
    [
        new("200000", "200K window"),
        new("1000000", "1M window")
    ];

    private void SelectWindow(string? value)
    {
        if (long.TryParse(value, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Integer, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var window) && window > 0)
        {
            _window = window;
        }
    }

    private static string FormatTokens(decimal tokens) => $"~{MetricFormat.Compact(tokens)}";

    /// <summary>
    /// One host's window, in the order it reads: what it carries every session,
    /// what the picked paths added, what came off this machine, and everything
    /// this app cannot see.
    /// <para>The bar drops a part worth nothing and works the shares out of what
    /// is left, so the matched band simply is not there until a path is picked —
    /// no empty segment, and no branch here to avoid one.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MetricPart> WindowParts(Func<InstructionLoadingRow, InstructionHostReach> host, string hostName)
    {
        var always = Tokens(LoadedBytes(host, InstructionReach.Always));
        var matched = Tokens(LoadedBytes(host, InstructionReach.OnMatch));
        var machine = MachineTotal(hostName).EstimatedTokens;

        return
        [
            new MetricPart("Repository, every session", always),
            new MetricPart("Matched by the selected paths", matched),
            new MetricPart("From this machine", machine),
            new MetricPart("Messages, tools and the rest", Math.Max(0, _window - always - matched - machine))
        ];
    }

    /// <summary>Whether a host has any rule a path could switch on at all — as
    /// opposed to having one that this selection happens to miss. The two look
    /// identical on the bar and are not the same fact.</summary>
    private bool HasNoPathScopedRules(Func<InstructionLoadingRow, InstructionHostReach> host) =>
        !Comparison.Rows.Any(row => host(row).Reach == InstructionReach.OnMatch);

    private long LoadedBytes(Func<InstructionLoadingRow, InstructionHostReach> host, InstructionReach reach) =>
        Comparison.Rows
            .Where(row => host(row).Loaded && host(row).Reach == reach)
            .Sum(row => row.Size.Bytes);

    private static long Tokens(long bytes) => (long)Math.Round(bytes / 4d, MidpointRounding.AwayFromZero);

    private List<InstructionLoadingRow> OneSided => [.. Comparison.Rows.Where(row => row.IsOneSided)];

    /// <summary>The path as the rules are written, not as this file system spells
    /// it — the reader is checking it against an instruction file, and those say
    /// <c>.github/instructions</c> whatever the machine underneath does.</summary>
    private static string FilePath(InstructionLoadingRow row) =>
        row.Document.RelativePath.Replace('\\', '/');

    private static object RowKey(InstructionLoadingRow row) => row.Document.RelativePath;

    private static string? RowCssClass(InstructionLoadingRow row) =>
        row.IsOneSided ? "instruction-reach__row--one-sided" : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// A picked row, drawn as picked.
    /// <para>The tree's default class carries no selected state — it applies
    /// whatever this returns and nothing else, so <c>IsNodeSelected</c> alone
    /// reaches <c>aria-selected</c> and stops there. Without this a picked row
    /// looked exactly like an unpicked one while the row under the pointer looked
    /// picked, which is worse than no highlight at all.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string PickedClass(TreeNode node) =>
        IsPathPicked(node) ? "knowledge-menu__item knowledge-menu__item--active" : "knowledge-menu__item";

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DataTableColumn> MachineColumns =
    [
        new("Location"),
        new("Assistant"),
        new("Size")
    ];

    private static object MachineKey(UserContextSource source) => source.Path + source.Label;

    private InstructionLoadTotals MachineTotal(string host) => UserContextSources.Total(UserContext, host);

    /// <summary>What a location that loads nothing is. Three different answers,
    /// because a folder nobody created, a folder somebody emptied, and a folder
    /// this app could not open are three different things to know.</summary>
    private static string MachineLabel(UserContextSource source) => source.State switch
    {
        UserContextState.Empty => "Empty",
        UserContextState.Unreadable => "Unreadable",
        _ => "Not there"
    };

    private static string MachineExplain(UserContextSource source) => source.State switch
    {
        UserContextState.Empty => "The location exists and holds nothing, so nothing is loaded from it.",
        UserContextState.Unreadable => "The location is there but could not be read, so what it loads is unknown.",
        _ => "Nothing at this location on this machine."
    };

    private static string Kilobytes(long bytes) => (bytes / 1024d).ToString("0.#");

    /// <summary>The tile's second line: how the figure above it is made up.</summary>
    private static string Breakdown(InstructionLoadTotals totals) =>
        totals.Files == 0
            ? "nothing loaded"
            : $"{totals.Files} {(totals.Files == 1 ? "file" : "files")}, {totals.Lines} lines, ~{totals.EstimatedTokens} tokens est.";

    private static string Label(InstructionHostReach reach) => reach.Reach switch
    {
        InstructionReach.Always => "Always",
        InstructionReach.OnMatch => "On match",
        InstructionReach.Linked => "Linked",
        _ => "Not read"
    };

    /// <summary>An On match that a picked path turned on is drawn as the loaded
    /// thing it now is, rather than staying the conditional it was.</summary>
    private static string Slug(InstructionHostReach reach) => reach.Reach switch
    {
        InstructionReach.Always => "always",
        InstructionReach.OnMatch => reach.Loaded ? "matched" : "on-match",
        InstructionReach.Linked => "linked",
        _ => "not-read"
    };

    /// <summary>What the badge means, for a pointer. The scope sits beside the
    /// badge as text, so this says why rather than repeating where.</summary>
    private static string Explain(InstructionHostReach reach) => reach.Reach switch
    {
        InstructionReach.Always when reach.Detail == "imported" =>
            "Pulled in by an import from a file that loads every session.",
        InstructionReach.Always => "Loaded into every session.",
        InstructionReach.OnMatch when reach.Loaded => "A selected path falls in this scope, so it is loaded.",
        InstructionReach.OnMatch => "Loaded when a path in this scope is worked on.",
        InstructionReach.Linked => "Named by a file that is loaded, but not loaded itself.",
        _ => "This host has no path to the file."
    };
}
